---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import AuthorCard from '../components/AuthorCard.astro';
---
<Base
  title="About"
  description="Casey Gager — cybersecurity practitioner, researcher, homelab operator."
>
  <div class="max-w-article mx-auto px-6 py-16">

    <header class="mb-12">
      <p class="lane-tag mb-4">About</p>
      <h1 class="font-serif text-title text-text-primary mb-4">Casey Gager</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="article-body">

      <p>
        About pages are hard to write without sounding like an asshole. I'll try
        to be honest instead.
      </p>

      <p>
        I work in cybersecurity. Have for a long time. I help enterprise security
        teams think through architecture -- identity, access control, Zero Trust,
        AI governance. I've had these conversations hundreds of times. I'm good
        at them.
      </p>

      <p>
        Here's what I don't say in those conversations: I'm not always sure I'm
        right. The gap between what I recommend and what's actually true is
        something I think about more than I let on. The frameworks I cite were
        written by committees. The vendor capabilities I describe come from
        documentation, not from running the thing. There's a version of what I
        do that's well-credentialed guessing, and I've been honest enough with
        myself to admit that bothers me.
      </p>

      <p>
        So I built it. Prometheus is a personal AI system running on a homelab
        K3s cluster in my house -- multi-persona orchestration, episodic memory,
        runtime authorization, autonomous build agents, affect sensing. The same
        architecture I'd spec for a client. Not a proof of concept. Production,
        running against my actual work.
      </p>

      <p>
        Building it has been clarifying in ways I didn't expect. A personal AI
        system is a self-portrait. The gaps in my reasoning don't stay abstract --
        they become broken services and wrong outputs. The things I care about
        become design decisions I didn't realize I was making. My blind spots show
        up in the system's behavior before I've noticed them in myself. You can't
        build something like this without it being deeply personal, and I didn't
        fully understand that until I was already in it.
      </p>

      <p>
        This site is where I write about what I find. Original research on
        problems I think the field is getting wrong. Practitioner takes that I'd
        give to a peer over coffee but not in a vendor presentation. Build logs
        for how Prometheus works and what it cost me to get it there.
      </p>

      <p>
        My thinking changes. I'll try to be honest when it does. I'm more
        interested in understanding things than being right about them, which
        sounds good on paper and is genuinely uncomfortable in practice. If
        something I've written is wrong, I want to know.
      </p>

    </div>

    <AuthorCard />

  </div>
</Base>
